                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Know yourself first`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Before you lead anyone, read your own wiring. Your profile is <strong>decisive, task-focused, low-disclosure</strong> — push hard, communicate facts, move on. In a 144-year relationship culture, that instinct reads as <em>cold and rushed</em> and is the exact style most likely to trigger resistance. The trap is to skip this and jump to the plan, leading authentically by leading <em>unconsciously</em>. Pollack's approach starts the other way round: interpret the profile, name the gap between your default and what this change needs, and choose the two EI behaviours to consciously dial up.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Coach the resistant anchor`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Your most respected operations manager — <strong>22 years, 140 people, the cultural anchor</strong> — has just told you privately: “I've seen three of these. They end with people I hired losing their jobs.” If she resists, ~60% of the division follows her; if you bring her on board, she becomes your strongest advocate. The trap is to meet her directively — “this is happening, get on board” — and drive her resistance underground. Diagnose what her resistance is really <em>about</em>, choose a coaching intervention over a command, and decide what you can authentically commit to her.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Frame the change message`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The division-wide launch is in days. You're speaking to 600 people who <strong>half-believe the mutual will protect them and half-fear it won't</strong>. Choose the frame, decide how much hard truth to lead with — 8 branches, 180 reshaped roles — versus reassurance, and align it with your authentic voice. The trap on one side is mistaking <em>authentic</em> for <em>unfiltered</em>: blunt, low-empathy honesty with a noble label. The trap on the other is over-reassuring and hiding the hard truth — which is fatal in a trust culture the moment reality leaks.`,
