                    coach_r3_overreassure:  `<strong>You've soft-pedalled the closures to protect trust.</strong> “No one needs to worry” feels kind now — but the closure list will leak, and then you're the leader who wasn't straight with them. In a trust culture that's fatal. How much hard truth should you have led with?`,
                    coach_r3_brutal:        `<strong>Leading with almost pure hard truth and no reassurance is candour without care.</strong> In this culture, all facts and no empathy reads as cold and lands as a threat, not honesty. Lead with the truth — but pair it with the renewal and the reskilling commitment.`,
                    coach_r3_disciplined:   `<strong>That's disciplined authenticity.</strong> True to your direct style, but consciously empathetic and disclosing — so a loss-and-renewal frame lands as genuine rather than scripted. You stayed yourself <em>and</em> dialled up the EI this moment needed.`,
                    coach_r3_scripted:      `<strong>A polished, scripted speech that hides your discomfort lands as scripted.</strong> People in a trust culture can tell. Authentic isn't a Comms-written performance — it's your real voice, disciplined to be empathetic. Owning your own words, imperfect, beats a flawless script.`,
                    coach_r3_unfiltered:    `<strong>“I'm just being authentic” is an EI failure with a noble label.</strong> Being blunt about 180 reshaped roles with no empathy isn't honesty — it's venting your defaults. Authentic leadership is self-aware, value-consistent behaviour. Stay true to yourself <em>and</em> dial up the empathy this moment needs.`,
                    coach_r4_own:           `<strong>Owning the leak fast is the trust-protective move.</strong> Confirming the facts, holding the frame and explaining the reskilling commitment openly turns a breach into a demonstration that you're straight with people. That's how candour buys trust back.`,
                    coach_r4_deflect:       `<strong>Calling the leaked list “speculation” is the move that ends trust.</strong> They've seen the list. Deny what they can read and you're no longer just changing the firm — you're the leader who wasn't honest, in the one culture where that can't be recovered cheaply.`,
                    coach_r4_clampdown:     `<strong>Hunting the leaker tells the division exactly who you are under pressure.</strong> Controlling the message instead of being straight confirms every fear the anchor voiced. You'll spend trust you can't get back chasing a leak that's already public.`,
                    coach_r4_invest_full:   `<strong>That's the visible “we look after our own” move.</strong> Committing the full envelope to reskilling and redeployment, right after owning the leak, proves the 144-year promise with money, not words. This is the single action that most moves adoption and trust together.`,
                    coach_r4_underfund:     `<strong>You trimmed reskilling to hit a number, so some of the 180 are let go.</strong> In 144 years this mutual has never made a compulsory redundancy. You've just spent the firm's deepest cultural promise to save a few hundred thousand — and every remaining employee now knows the “we look after our own” story is over. Was it worth the soul of the place?`,
                    coach_r4_sideline:      `<strong>Routing the change around the anchor confirms her doubt.</strong> She's still watching, and so are her 140. Sideline her now and you waste the one relationship that could carry adoption — and hand the doubters their leader.`,
                    coach_r4_announce:      `<strong>“Change communicated” is not change adopted.</strong> Your all-hands had applause and good energy, but the board measures behavioural take-up — and weeks later people quietly keep the old way. You announced a change; you didn't lead one. Where's the adoption?`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Spinning the number in front of the board is the short game.</strong> They'll see the resistance you downplayed the moment it surfaces, and then you've lost their trust as well as the division's. An honest, evidenced read is the only one that survives contact with reality.`,
                    coach_r4_leak_hidden:   `<strong>The hard truth you hid in your launch just leaked.</strong> The soft-pedalled closures are now public, and in a trust culture the leader who wasn't straight pays in collapsing trust — exactly the asset the whole change runs on. Candour up front would have cost less than this.`,
